---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
export const prerender = false;

let allPosts = await getCollection("blog");
allPosts = allPosts.filter((post) => !post.data.draft);

import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Hero from "../sections/Hero.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import PostCard from "../components/PostCard.astro";
---

<Layout description="What I Do.">
    <Hero />

    <Section id="about-me">
        <div class="about-content">
            <header class="about-header">
                <h2 class="h1">About Me</h2>
            </header>
            <div class="about-stats">
                <div class="stat">
                    <span class="stat-value">B.Sc.</span>
                    <span class="stat-label">In progress</span>
                </div>
                <div class="stat">
                    <span class="stat-value">2+</span>
                    <span class="stat-label">Years in industry</span>
                </div>
            </div>
            <p>
                My relationship with technology started long before I wrote a
                line of code. I took an interest in programming in high school.
                But, wasn't serious about it until after my first class at
                community college, then YouTube, channels like Traversy Media
                and Academind, until it stuck. I'm most at home building things
                people actually use, getting into the infrastructure underneath
                them, and picking up new tools whenever something interesting
                crosses my path.
            </p>
            <p>
                Some of the most valuable lines of code I've experienced were
                ones I never thought about—iOS on my first iPod, the ones
                keeping YouTube running through countless hours of watching
                everything from tutorials to nonsense. Written by people I'll
                never meet, they shaped more of who I am than I realized at the
                time. That's still true today—the ones on my Linux installation
                that let me tinker with my development environment, and the ones
                Strava's engineering team wrote that gave me an outlet through
                fitness and introduced me to some of the best people in my life.
                That's the bar I aim to hold myself to.
            </p>

            <p>
                Based in Denver, I spend my free time running, cycling, and
                hunting down good coffee shops neighborhood by neighborhood. I
                think the way I work and the way I move through the world are
                the same thing: figure out how it works, put in the miles, and
                stay curious.
            </p>
        </div>
    </Section>
    <Section class="contrast" id="recent-posts">
        <header class="group">
            <h2>Recent Posts</h2>
        </header>
        <ul class="grid" style="padding-left: 0;">
            {allPosts.map((post) => <PostCard {post} />)}
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Layout>

<style>
    .about-content {
        display: flex;
        padding: 1rem 0;
        flex-direction: column;
        gap: var(--app-spacing);
    }
    .about-stats {
        display: flex;
        gap: var(--app-spacing);
        border-top: var(--app-border-width) solid var(--app-border-color);
        padding-top: 0.2rem;
    }

    .stat {
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        font-family: "Host Grotesk Variable", sans-serif;
        font-weight: 800;
        letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    }

    .stat-value {
        font-size: 2rem;
        color: var(--orange--lighten);
    }

    .stat-label {
        font-size: 0.8rem;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        color: var(--gray-200);
    }
</style>
